<node id="1232778" title="Re^8: Getting for() to accept a tied array in one statement" created="2019-04-18 16:22:03" updated="2019-04-18 16:22:03" _doctext_mode="strict"><type id="11">note</type>
<author id="830549">haukex</author>
<doctext field_name="doctext">&lt;blockquote&gt;&lt;i&gt;My point is, I cannot know what $art is, so to me this is a big no no: &lt;c&gt;my @ar-deref = @{$art};&lt;/c&gt;&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Yes, I agree &lt;i&gt;that&lt;/i&gt; is bad (unless &lt;c&gt;$art&lt;/c&gt; [doc://overload]s &lt;c&gt;@{}&lt;/c&gt;), but in the code you showed [id://1232653|here], &lt;c&gt;for (tie @ary, "My::Class")&lt;/c&gt;, there is no such "bad" de-reference of the return value of [doc://tie] happening, not even an implicit one. &lt;c&gt;for (tie @ary, "My::Class") { print "$x\n" }&lt;/c&gt; will just print something like &lt;c&gt;My::Class=HASH(0x...)&lt;/c&gt; - as [perlancar] [id://1232649|said], it's just looping over a one-element list, the return value of [doc://tie], and &lt;i&gt;not&lt;/i&gt; the elements of &lt;c&gt;@{$art}&lt;/c&gt;, as you seemed to be worried about.&lt;/p&gt;
</doctext>
